on date and customer. Having both in one index can allow index-only scans or better merge joins.\n\n4. **`catalog_sales(cs_sold_date_sk, cs_bill_customer_sk)`** \u2014 Q4, Q11, Q38, Q74 similarly need date + bill_customer joining. Confirmed the planner is already splitting these as two lookups \u2014 a composite may reduce I/O.\n\n5. **`web_sales(ws_sold_date_sk, ws_bill_customer_sk)`** \u2014 Same rationale as above for Q4, Q11, Q38, Q74 web-sales arms.\n\n6. **`store_returns(sr_returned_date_sk, sr_customer_sk, sr_store_sk)`** \u2014 Q1 CTE aggregates store_returns by customer and store after filtering by year. A composite reduces the sequential scan of the returns table.\n\n7. **`customer_address(ca_gmt_offset, ca_address_sk)`** \u2014 Q33, Q56, Q60 filter on gmt_offset and then join on ca_address_sk. Plans showed this was already partially used via tmp index; formalizing saves overhead.\n\n8. **`item(i_manager_id, i_item_sk)`** \u2014 Q3, Q42, Q52, Q55, Q71 filter items by manager_id then join to sales on i_item_sk. Composite allows index-only lookup.\n\n9. **`item(i_category, i_class, i_item_sk)`** \u2014 Q6, Q12, Q20, Q33, Q53, Q56, Q60, Q63, Q98 filter item by category/class then join on item_sk. Reduces filtering cost significantly.\n\n10. **`customer(c_current_addr_sk, c_customer_sk, c_current_cdemo_sk)`** \u2014 Q10, Q35, Q69 start from customer_address filtered on county/state then join to customer on c_current_addr_sk and further join on cdemo_sk. This composite avoids extra lookups.\n\n11. **`date_dim(d_date, d_date_sk)`** \u2014 Q5, Q12, Q16, Q20, Q21, Q32, Q37, Q40 filter on d_date range. Plans confirmed `idx_date_dim_date` exists; included for completeness of date-based filtering.\n\n12. **`date_dim(d_year, d_moy, d_date_sk)`** \u2014 Confirmed already existing as `idx_date_dim_year_moy`; retained in recommendation for clarity.\n\n13. **`store_sales(ss_quantity, ss_ext_discount_amt, ss_net_paid_inc_tax)`** \u2014 Q9 repeatedly scans store_sales filtering on ss_quantity ranges to compute averages. Plans showed a tmp index `tmp_idx_store_sales_42a93dd4` is being used; a proper covering index including the aggregated columns enables index-only scans across all 5 buckets, significantly reducing total cost from ~700K.",
